.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt TAR 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm tar
.Nd manipulate tape archives
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Brq Fl c | Fl r | Fl u
.Op Fl aHhLnPvz
.Op Fl b Ar blocks
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Op Fl f Ar archive
.Op Fl s Ar /old/new/
.Op Fl T Ar file
.Op Fl X Ar file
.Op Fl -exclude Ar pattern
.Op Fl -zstd
.Op Ar file | Fl C Ar dir ...
.Nm
.Brq Fl t | Fl x
.Op Fl kmOoPpqv
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Op Fl f Ar archive
.Op Fl s Ar /old/new/
.Op Fl T Ar file
.Op Fl X Ar file
.Op Fl -exclude Ar pattern
.Op Fl -strip-components Ar n
.Op Ar pattern ...
.Nm
.Ar bundled-flags Op Ar args
.Op Ar file | pattern ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes files into an archive, lists an archive's members and extracts them.
The first option names what to do:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c
Create a new archive of each
.Ar file ,
and of everything in it if it is a directory.
.It Fl r
Append the files to the end of an existing archive, which must be a file and must not be
compressed.
.It Fl t
List the members of the archive, or those that a
.Ar pattern
selects.
.It Fl u
As
.Fl r ,
but only files that are not in the archive yet or are newer than their copy in it.
.It Fl x
Extract the members of the archive, or those that a
.Ar pattern
selects.
.El
.Pp
For compatibility, the first argument may be a word of option letters without a
.Sq Fl ,
as in
.Ql tar xvf archive.tar ;
the arguments of the letters
.Cm b , C , f , I , s , T
and
.Cm X
in it follow it in the same order.
.Pp
A
.Ar pattern
selects a member if it matches the member's whole name or a leading part of it ending
before a
.Sq / ,
so a directory's name selects everything in it.
In a pattern,
.Ql * ,
.Ql \&?
and
.Ql [...]
are as in the shell, except that they also match
.Ql / .
A pattern that selects nothing is reported as an error at the end.
.Pp
The other options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a , Fl -auto-compress
With
.Fl c ,
choose the compression from the archive's name:
gzip for
.Ql .tgz ,
.Ql .taz
and
.Ql .gz ,
zstd for
.Ql .tzst
and
.Ql .zst .
.It Fl b Ar blocks , Fl -blocking-factor Ar blocks
Write records of
.Ar blocks
512-byte blocks (default 20); the archive is padded to a whole record.
.It Fl C Ar dir , Fl -directory Ar dir , Fl -cd Ar dir
Change to
.Ar dir
first.
With
.Fl c ,
.Fl r
and
.Fl u ,
.Fl C Ar dir
may also appear among the
.Ar file
operands, changing directory before the files after it.
With
.Fl t
and
.Fl x ,
it takes effect after the archive is opened.
.It Fl f Ar archive , Fl -file Ar archive
Use
.Ar archive ;
.Sq Fl
is the standard input or output.
.It Fl H
Follow symbolic links named on the command line.
.It Fl h , Fl L , Fl -dereference
Follow all symbolic links, archiving what they point to.
.It Fl k , Fl -keep-old-files
Do not replace existing files when extracting.
.It Fl m , Fl -modification-time
Do not restore modification times when extracting.
.It Fl n , Fl -no-recursion
Do not descend into directories.
.It Fl O , Fl -to-stdout
Extract members' data to the standard output instead of to files.
.It Fl o , Fl -no-same-owner
Do not restore owners when extracting, even as root.
.It Fl P , Fl -absolute-paths
Keep a leading
.Sq /
in names, allow
.Ql ..
components, and follow symbolic links on the way to a member.
See
.Sx SECURITY .
.It Fl p , Fl -preserve-permissions
Restore permissions whole, without the umask, including the set-user-ID,
set-group-ID and sticky bits.
This is the default for root.
.It Fl q , Fl -fast-read
With patterns, stop at the first member each of them selects.
.It Fl s Ar /old/new/ Ns Op Ar flags
Rename members, the first time the basic regular expression
.Ar old
matches in a name, to
.Ar new ,
in which
.Ql &
is the matched text and
.Ql \e1
to
.Ql \e9
are its subexpressions.
Any character may take the place of the
.Sq / .
The
.Ar flags
are
.Cm g ,
to replace every match,
.Cm p ,
to print each renaming, and
.Cm H ,
.Cm R ,
.Cm S ,
to leave hard link targets, member names and symbolic link targets alone
.Pf ( Cm h ,
.Cm r ,
.Cm s
restore them).
With several
.Fl s ,
the first one that matches is applied.
A name renamed to nothing is left out.
.It Fl -strip-components Ar n
Remove the first
.Ar n
components of each member's name when extracting; members with no more are left out.
.It Fl T Ar file , Fl I Ar file , Fl -files-from Ar file
Read names (or, with
.Fl t
and
.Fl x ,
patterns) from
.Ar file ,
one per line, before those on the command line.
A line
.Ql -C Ar dir
changes directory.
.It Fl U
Accepted for compatibility: an existing file is always removed before its replacement is
created.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
With
.Fl c ,
.Fl r ,
.Fl u
and
.Fl x ,
name each member on the standard error.
With
.Fl t ,
list members as
.Xr ls 1
.Fl l
does.
.It Fl X Ar file , Fl -exclude-from Ar file
Read exclusion patterns from
.Ar file ,
one per line.
.It Fl -exclude Ar pattern
Leave out files and members that
.Ar pattern
matches: the whole name, or a part of it between
.Sq /
characters, so
.Ql --exclude '*.o'
leaves out object files at any depth.
.It Fl -format Ar format
Write the archive as
.Cm ustar ,
failing for whatever that cannot hold,
.Cm pax ,
with an extended header for every member, or
.Cm restricted
(the default): ustar, with a pax extended header only before a member it cannot hold.
.It Fl -null
Names in a
.Fl T
or
.Fl X
file are separated by NUL bytes, not newlines.
.It Fl -numeric-owner
Restore owners by number, ignoring the names in the archive.
.It Fl -same-owner , Fl -no-same-permissions
Restore owners; apply the umask.
.It Fl z , Fl -gzip
Compress the archive with gzip.
.It Fl -zstd
Compress the archive with
.Xr zstd 1 .
.El
.Pp
When reading,
.Nm
recognises gzip and zstd compression by itself.
Archives in the ustar, pax, GNU and old V7 formats are read, with pax extended and global
headers, GNU long names and base-256 numbers.
.Pp
When creating, directories are walked in name order.
A file with several links is stored once, its other names as hard links to the first.
Sockets and the archive itself are left out.
Without
.Fl P ,
a leading
.Sq /
or
.Ql ../
is removed from names, with a note.
.Sh SECURITY
Extraction assumes an archive was made to do harm.
Without
.Fl P :
.Bl -bullet
.It
A leading
.Sq /
is removed from member names and hard link targets, and a member whose name or hard link
target has a
.Ql ..
component is refused.
.It
Each directory on the way to a member is opened without following symbolic links, so a
member is never written through one: an archive holding a link
.Ql x
to
.Pa /etc
and then a file
.Ql x/passwd
gets
.Dq Cannot extract through symlink x .
.It
An existing file is removed, not overwritten, and the new one is created exclusively; a
symbolic link in its place is replaced, never followed.
.It
Owners are set only by root, and the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits only on a file that
belongs to the archive's owner.
.El
.Pp
Names shown in listings and messages have control characters written as octal escapes.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TAPE
.It Ev TAPE
The archive when there is no
.Fl f .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /sbin/zstd
.It Pa /sbin/zstd
Used to compress and expand zstd archives.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Errors with single members do not stop the others; they are reported as they happen and in
the exit status.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Archive a directory, compressed:
.Dl $ tar -czf src.tgz src
.Pp
List it at length:
.Dl $ tar -tvf src.tgz
.Pp
Extract only its manual pages into
.Pa /tmp :
.Dl $ tar -xf src.tgz -C /tmp 'src/*.1'
.Pp
Copy a tree, keeping permissions:
.Dl $ tar -cf - -C /usr/local share | tar -xpf - -C /mnt
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gzip 1 ,
.Xr zstd 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The archive formats are the ustar and pax interchange formats of
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq),
which specifies them through
.Xr pax 1 ;
.Nm
itself is not in POSIX.
The options are those of bsdtar.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v7 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
.Sh CAVEATS
With no
.Fl f
and no
.Ev TAPE ,
the archive is the standard input or output; the BSDs' default is a tape drive
.Pf ( Pa /dev/sa0
or
.Pa /dev/rst0 ) ,
which OxideBSD does not have.
.Pp
bzip2
.Pq Fl j ,
xz
.Pq Fl J
and
.Xr compress 1
.Pq Fl Z
data are refused, as are sparse members.
Interactive renaming
.Pq Fl w ,
ACLs, extended attributes and file flags are not provided.
